# encdiff corpus: 64-bit data16 branch rows.
#
# The x86-64 encoder treats the `data16` word on a direct near branch as a
# DEAD prefix byte (Intel SDM: operand-size prefixes have no effect on near
# branches in 64-bit mode; hardware-proven — the decoder consumes the full
# 4-byte displacement after 66 e9/e8/0f 8x). lccc emits `66 <normal-row>`:
# near targets relax to `66 eb/7x rel8` (GAS parity), far and external
# targets keep the rel32 row + R_X86_64_PLT32.
#
# GAS 2.47 emits the truncated rel16 field for far/external targets
# (66 e9/e8/0f 8x + R_X86_64_PC16) — encodings that MIS-EXECUTE on real
# hardware (the decoder reads two bytes past the instruction). encdiff
# therefore reports these rows as ORACLE-INVALID, not as a length race:
# lccc's longer form is the shortest VALID encoding.
#
# These rows cannot live in tests/asm-diff/*.casefile: the asm-diff
# contract is byte-equality with GNU as, and GAS's bytes here are the
# invalid ones. Run with:
#   scripts/encdiff.py --lccc ./target/fastbuild/lccc \
#       --file tests/encdiff-corpus/data16-branches-64.insn
#
# Local labels are scaffolded by encdiff's local_label_scaffold (the
# matching `N:` lands immediately after the instruction); a `+N` addend
# pushes the scaffolded target out of disp8 range to exercise the far
# (rel32) rows.
data16 jmp foo
data16 je foo
data16 jne foo
data16 call foo
data16 jmp 1f
data16 je 1f
data16 jne 1f
data16 call 1f
data16 jmp 1f+300
data16 je 1f+300
data16 jne 1f+300
data16 call 1f+300
data16 jmp 1b
data16 je 1b
data16 jne 1b
data16 call 1b
